<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    A global the program only moves through floating-point registers is a
    float (option floatglobals, kuna_floatglobals.rs). The script loads three
    in-tree linked images of floatglobal.c (source beside them): gcc -O2 for
    x86-64, gcc -O2 for AArch64 and for hard-float ARM.

    fy copies its double parameter into gd and ff its float into gf, and no
    other code touches either but through a float register. With the option
    off every global refuses the parameter vote and the parameter prints as an
    integer the convention passes in a general register; on, the whole-program
    scan finds gd and gf float-only and the parameters are floats. set_gpun
    memcpys a float into gpun, which use_gpun adds to as an integer, and clearz
    zeroes the double gz with an integer store: both globals refuse, and
    set_gpun and setz keep their integer parameters. Hard-float ARM drops fy's
    d0 parameter (armfloatargs off), so only ff is asked there.

    floatglobal_pun.c's fy stores its double into gd and also reads gd's bits
    as an integer where the whole-program scan cannot see it: in a case of a
    jump table no range check bounds, or through a pointer spilled among more
    stack slots than the scan follows. The function's own code refuses the
    global, so the parameter keeps its integer type and the read prints no
    conversion of gd.

    floatglobal_index.c's functions store a double into a global and read or
    write its bits as an integer through an index from its address, which
    the scan cannot see either: a float there printed `(&gda)[a1]` as a
    double element converted to the integer. From a declared scalar (ixa,
    lv0, ixs) any index refuses; from a struct or an array (fld, neg) the
    integer load through the index refuses. put indexes a double array only
    as doubles and keeps its double. gcc -O2 and -O0 for x86-64, gcc -O2 for
    AArch64 (an object, whose data no Symbol sizes).
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
